# Price elasticity: how much less do people buy?
subtitle: "How one number measures how strongly buyers react to a price, why substitutes and time change it, and when a fare rise loses money."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | an informative, warm economist reading his own short book aloud; steady, natural pace","take":"lines"}

chapter: "What elasticity is"

## What elasticity measures
domain: {"x":[0,60],"y":[0,100]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

    axes ax x_label Rides y_label Price hidden true
    curve d #b5482e expr "90 - x" x_from 5 x_to 60 hidden true
    label lD "D" above-right #b5482e attach_to d hidden true
    math e1 tex "\\frac{-33\\%}{+17\\%} \\approx -2" size 34 x 720 y 560 hidden true

Everyone knows that when a price goes up, people buy less. The interesting question is how much less.
    draw ax

To see it, take a made-up small town and the demand for its bus rides.
    draw d lD

At sixty kroner a ride, people take thirty rides a day.
    dot pA at.on d at.x 30 guides.values true

Now raise the fare to seventy, and the rides drop to twenty.
    dot pB at.on d at.x 20 guides.values true
    arrow mv pA -> pB #8a5fa8
    label lmv "price up" above-right #8a5fa8 attach_to mv

That jump, from one point to the other, is what elasticity turns into a single number.
    write "**Elasticity** — how strongly quantity reacts to a price change"

It compares the two changes in percent, how much quantity moved, divided by how much the price moved.
    write id formula text "$$\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\%\\,\\Delta Q}{\\%\\,\\Delta P}$$"

So quantity sits on top, and price underneath.
    highlight formula effect circle

    write "Bus rides: $\\dfrac{-33\\%}{+17\\%} \\approx -2$"

For our buses, the fare rose about seventeen percent while rides fell by a third, which gives an elasticity of about minus two.
    draw e1

    pause 1

## Two extremes
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}
vars: {"k":0.25}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

Minus two is a big reaction. But some goods barely react at all.
    axes ax x_label Quantity y_label Price

    write "- **Inelastic**, $|\\varepsilon| < 1$: insulin, petrol"

Insulin is the classic case. Double its price, and diabetics still need the same dose, so the curve stands almost upright.
    curve insulin_d #b5482e expr "100 - 4*x" x_from 5 x_to 22
    label lSteep "insulin" right #b5482e attach_to insulin_d

Such a steep line looks dramatic. Does that make it the elastic one?
    write id guess text "Steep = elastic?" temp true

No, it is the other way round. Steep means quantity hardly moves, and that is inelastic.
    highlight guess effect strike

    write "- **Elastic**, $|\\varepsilon| > 1$: one brand of cola"

Compare one brand of cola. Raise its price a little, and buyers simply reach for another brand, so its curve lies almost flat.
    curve cola_d #2f6b8f expr "47.5 - k*(x - 30)" x_from 5 x_to 60
    label lFlat "one cola brand" above-right #2f6b8f attach_to cola_d

Now take cola's rivals off the shelf, one by one, and watch its curve tilt up toward insulin's.
    animate k 1.5 duration 3 ghost ["cola_d"]

Put them back, and it flattens again.
    animate k 0.25 duration 2.2

    point at.ref formula

Back in our formula, that is exactly why. A steep curve means a small change on top, and so a small elasticity.
    text steepnote "steep → small %ΔQ → small ε" #b5482e x 640 y 620 font_size 28

In short, what decides it is whether buyers have somewhere else to go.
    write id substitutes text "> Substitutes decide it."

    highlight substitutes part Substitutes

    pause 0.6

chapter: "Measuring and using it"

## The midpoint method
domain: {"x":[0,50],"y":[40,80]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

Back to the buses, because our minus two hides a snag.
    axes ax x_label Rides y_label Price
    curve d #b5482e expr "90 - x" x_from 10 x_to 50
    dot pA at.on d at.x 30 guides.values true
    dot pB at.on d at.x 20 guides.values true
    parallel true

Going up from sixty, the fare rose seventeen percent. But going down from seventy, the same step is only fourteen.
    write "60 → 70 or 70 → 60? A % change depends on where you start."

The usual fix is to measure both changes from the midpoint, the average of the two.
    write "$$\\%\\Delta P = \\frac{P_2 - P_1}{(P_1 + P_2)/2}$$"

    dot pM #8a5fa8 at.on d at.x 25 guides.values true
    label lM "midpoint" right #8a5fa8 attach_to pM

For the fares, that is sixty-five.
    write text "65 = halfway between 60 and 70" temp true

    write "$$\\%\\Delta P = \\frac{70 - 60}{65} \\approx 15.4\\%$$"

So ten kroner over sixty-five makes about fifteen percent.
    text cP "%ΔP = 10 / 65 ≈ +15 %" x 720 y 640 font_size 28

    write "$$\\%\\Delta Q = \\frac{20 - 30}{25} = -40\\%$$"

The rides get the same treatment, minus ten over twenty-five, which is minus forty percent.
    text cQ "%ΔQ = −10 / 25 = −40 %" x 720 y 585 font_size 28

    write id midpoint_result text "$$\\varepsilon = \\frac{-40\\%}{15.4\\%} \\approx -2.6$$"

Dividing one by the other then gives an elasticity of about minus two point six.
    text cE "ε ≈ −2.6" #b5482e x 720 y 520 font_size 36

    highlight midpoint_result effect box keep true

And because both changes start from the middle, you get the same number whichever way you go.
    write "The same answer in **both directions**."

    highlight pM

    pause 1

## Compute it
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

Doing this by hand every time gets tedious, so let the computer do it, in Python, running right here in your browser.
    ```python calc show above marks ["(a + b) / 2"]
    def pct(a, b):
        return (b - a) / ((a + b) / 2)

    p1, p2 = 60, 70
    q1, q2 = 30, 20
    print(round(pct(q1, q2) / pct(p1, p2), 2))
    ```

    write "A **function** for the formula: write it once, use it for any two points."

We start with a small function for the midpoint percentage change.
    draw calc_line_1 calc_line_2 parallel true

Its one idea is the average of the two values, underneath.
    draw calc_mark_1

Then we give it our two points, fares of sixty and seventy, and thirty and twenty rides.
    draw calc_line_4 calc_line_5 parallel true

With those in place, elasticity is just one change over the other, the rides on top and the fares underneath.
    draw calc_line_6

Run it, and out comes minus two point six, the same as by hand.
    draw calc_out

And once the function exists, any new pair of points is a single line.
    write "```python\nround(pct(30, 20) / pct(60, 70), 2)\n```\n```output\n-2.6\n```"

    pause 1

## Time changes it
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Petrol
      - Electricity
      - Cigarettes
    values:
      - [0.25, 0.15, 0.1]
      - [0.45, 0.35, 0.25]
      - [0.7, 0.55, 0.4]
    stage: 0
    value_labels: true
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 1
    y_label: Elasticity (size)

    text m1 "after 1 month" #8f887c x 700 y 640 font_size 36 hidden true

There is one more thing that bends these numbers, and it is time.
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 m1 parallel true

In the first month after a price rise, people mostly grumble and pay.
    write "Demand is more elastic in the **long run**: people find other ways."

    erase m1

    text m2 "after 1 year" #8f887c x 700 y 640 font_size 36

But give them a year, and they start to adjust, with car pools, a bike, a closer job.
    animate stage 1 duration 2.5 easing linear

    erase m2

    text m3 "after 5 years" #8f887c x 700 y 640 font_size 36

After five years every bar has grown, petrol most of all.
    animate stage 2 duration 2.5 easing linear

    write "| Good | 1 month | 5 years |\n|---|---|---|\n| Petrol | 0.25 | 0.7 |\n| Electricity | 0.15 | 0.55 |\n| Cigarettes | 0.1 | 0.4 |"

Every good tells the same story, the longer people have, the more they react.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

    write "*Illustrative numbers, not estimates.*"

    pause 1

## Why it matters for revenue
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,100]}
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

    axes ax x_label Rides y_label Price hidden true
    curve d #b5482e expr "90 - x" x_from 10 x_to 80 hidden true
    label lD "D" above-right #b5482e attach_to d hidden true
    polygon rA #d0865f points [[0,0],[30,0],[30,60],[0,60]] data true style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    dot pA at.on d at.x 30 guides.values true hidden true
    polygon rB #2f6b8f points [[0,0],[20,0],[20,70],[0,70]] data true style.fill #9cc3dc hidden true
    dot pB at.on d at.x 20 guides.values true hidden true
    polygon gain #4f7a3f points [[0,60],[20,60],[20,70],[0,70]] data true style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.8 hidden true
    polygon loss #b5482e points [[20,0],[30,0],[30,60],[20,60]] data true style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.6 hidden true
    text vA "60 × 30 = 1800" #d0865f x 700 y 620 font_size 30 hidden true
    text vB "70 × 20 = 1400" #2f6b8f x 700 y 570 font_size 30 hidden true
    text vL "−400 a day" #b5482e x 700 y 510 font_size 34 hidden true

All of this would be a curiosity if it did not decide something that matters to the bus company, its takings.
    draw ax d lD

    write "Revenue $= P \\times Q$"

Its revenue is the fare times the rides, which is this rectangle. Sixty times thirty, eighteen hundred kroner a day.
    draw rA pA vA

    write "$60 \\times 30 = 1800$ → $70 \\times 20 = 1400$"

After the fare rise the rectangle is taller, but much narrower. Seventy times twenty, only fourteen hundred.
    draw rB pB vB

    write text "1800 − 1400 = 400 lost" temp true

It gains the green strip, ten kroner more on each of twenty rides, but loses the red one, ten rides at sixty. So the rise costs the company four hundred kroner a day.
    draw gain loss vL

That is what elastic demand means in money. A higher price loses more in rides than it gains per ride.
    write id rule text "**Elastic** demand: raise the price, revenue **falls**"

So revenue falls, even though each ride now costs more.
    highlight rule part falls effect circle

    quiz "A ten percent fare rise cuts rides by four percent. What happens to revenue?"
        right: It rises. Rides fall less than the fare rose, so demand is inelastic here.
        + It rises
        * It falls
        * It stays the same

